speechtext:
As far as the operation of this act is concerned, the insurance aspects of it will operate exactly the same as the insurance aspects have operated in the past. As regards these new provisions that we are introducing today, I should like to read from the booklet called "Export Credits Insurance Corporation, what it is and how it operates", put out by the Exports Credits Corporation. At page 15 we find the following:
Financing for exports of capital goods where terms of payment extend beyond five years may be provided.
Then it goes on to say:
The guiding rules that determine the eligibility of transactions for financing are:
1.	that the extended credit terms are clearly necessary and justifiable;
2.	that the transaction in question gives promise of continuing export trade;

3.	that the Canadian content is of a higher order, in the range of 80 per cent or better;
4.	that there are demonstrable employment and industrial benefits for Canada deriving from the transaction;
5.	that the particular export transaction to be financed is substantial in value;
6.	that the foreign buyer and the country of purchase have a satisfactory credit standing.
The hon. member for Burnaby-Coquitlam asked me a question which I have not yet answered. His question was, are capital goods only eligible under this new legislation. The answer is yes; consumer goods are not covered under this new legislation.

